              <h2>What to Look for in a General Contractor in 2026</h2>

              <h3>Full-Service Home Renovation Expertise</h3>
              <p>Look for contractors who handle everything from initial design through final walkthrough. The best firms offer kitchens, baths, custom renovations, home additions, structural work, flooring, cabinetry, and all finishing touches — without farming out key work to unfamiliar subcontractors.</p>

              <h3>Cabinetry &amp; Storage Solutions That Fit Your Life</h3>
              <p>Cabinets are the backbone of your kitchen, mudroom, and home office. The right contractor doesn't just install boxes — they design storage around how you actually live. Ask to see kitchen storage flow, mudroom organization systems, and home office solutions in their completed project portfolio.</p>

              <h3>Attention to the Details</h3>
              <p>Decorative wall trim, perfect crown molding, and precise finishing work are what elevate a renovation from good to exceptional. Review contractors' finished work closely — the quality of their details tells you everything about their standards overall.</p>

              <h3>Additions That Seamlessly Blend In</h3>
              <p>Quality additions feel like they've been there all along. This requires matching exterior materials, rooflines, and interior flow — not just appending square footage. Ask specifically how they ensure new additions match existing construction.</p>

              <h3>Clear Communication &amp; Proven Reputation</h3>
              <ul class="post-list">
                <li>Clear scope of work and timelines established before contracts are signed</li>
                <li>Transparent pricing with no surprise fees or inflated change orders</li>
                <li>Regular construction updates — daily or weekly, not "when we remember"</li>
                <li>Prompt responsiveness to client questions throughout the project</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

